{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1126924-asking-for-suggestions-what-game-should-i-run-with-this-group","id":1126924,"title":"Asking for suggestions: what game should I run with this group?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-08-09T18:58:13-07:00","id":1126924,"title":"Asking for suggestions: what game should I run with this group?","content":"Hello there, I'm once again about to put on my Storyteller hat.<br /> <br /> Here's the deal: the group I'm about to run the game with is one where I started as player. Friends of friends, and all that. They met while playing D&amp;D, then one decided to make a campaign and that's where I got in. Now another one of them decided to run a parallel D&amp;D game to the one we already play in and, since they heard from my friends I'm an experienced game master and they're curious about these games that are so different from what they are used to (mostly meaning CoD, WoD and Call Of Cthulhu), asked me to come up with something as well.<br /> <br /> So, they're essentially having three D&amp;D games at the same time and I have to run something for them. The previous time I found myself in the position to run a game for D&amp;D lovers, I ran a CoD Dark Fantasy, but I feel like they have their fantasy covered.<br /> <br /> So, they clearly love their genre and yet I'd rather show them something a little different. What you think might be a good choice? I was thinking either a Innocents game (I'm good at those and they tend to be rule-light) or Changeling for its urban fantasy side, even if I never actually ran it (perhaps in Tokyo since all of them are shameless japanophiles). Exalted, perhaps. Any ideas? I'm kinda in the position to sell them on White Wolf/Onyx Path and, as a fan, I'm feeling the call of duty here.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/815620-cinder-s-comprehensive-collection-of-creations\" target=\"_blank\">Cinder's Comprehensive Collection of Creations</a> - Homebrew Hub<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1172470-cinder-writes-about-beast-the-primordial\" target=\"_blank\">I write about Beast: The Primordial a lot</a>","edited":{"userid":38,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/38-cinder","user":"Cinder","date":". 08-10-2017, 08:10 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/38-cinder","name":"Cinder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar38_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1408"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-09T19:11:55-07:00","id":1126929,"title":"","content":"<b>Exalted</b> isn't a bad choice, but like you say, they have their fantasy covered. <b>Demon: the Descent,</b> while it will require some setting exposition to grok, in my opinion excels at an episodic structure where the player characters can emerge into an action situation, accomplish a mission, and then recede to a steady state to seek the next big bang: run it like Leverage, probably with some contacts in a local agency or otherwise that can help highlight the big scores. Either iteration of <b>Vampire</b> can also decently accomplish action-heavy crime stories that I think would be accessible to players used to the D&amp;D mode of operation, if that exposition is too onerous or vampires are just an easier sell for them. <b>Hunter: the Vigil,</b> too, has a universal level of accessibility, so long as you keep the game to a cohesive pitch (what ties the hunters together? Bonds of certain compacts or conspiracies, or at tier one, what's the modus operandi they've settled into, what kind of hunt do they conduct and what resources do they call on?) rather than just tossing the book at them and letting them treat it like a catalogue of player options to order from freely.<br /> <br /> That latter bit is good advice all around: come up with <b>a couple central game pitches</b> or broad concepts (a Requiem game about a pressure-cooker city about to explode and being the wildcards there to make it happen, or a Descent game about a harried ring just out to create security for themselves, frustrated with humanity but gradually learning to get along with it), put them forth as a selection, and see what your group is more interested in. Recraft the pitch a bit around their expectations, and then have them create characters to match the pitch.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","name":"Stupid Loserman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar522_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1824"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-09T20:25:14-07:00","id":1126944,"title":"","content":"First, are you looking at running more of a one-shot to introduce them to a new system, or are you looking to run a game and then turn it into an extended campaign?<br /> <br /> I've introduced about a dozen different groups to White Wolf games over the years, and I've found I had the most luck with running people on a one shot of Wraith: the Oblivion. It's pretty easy to come up with a basic one-shot storyline. The PC's are in an airplane when it crashes, or at a sporting event when there's an explosion, or in a concert hall when there's a fire. They wake up confused in the Underworld and are immediately dropped into a conflict involving the Renegades and the Hierarchy, both of which have come to claim the souls or wraithly artifacts that have appeared. It's easy to throw in a bit of survival horror to spice things up if you're good at setting the right mood (one of my favorites involved the players having to travel through some old mining tunnels). <br /> <br /> This sort of thing can give players a good look at the WoD mechanics (rolling d10s, picking out target numbers, etc) in a setting that can be both eerily familiar and very different from other ones that they've played in.<br /> <br /> I think Changeling would be a good pick for more long-running campaign, especially if everyone in your group is animephiles. You can easily have them playing a troupe of high school changelings and play up the magical elements which can be pretty amusing. If you point them to the show Chuunibyou demo Koi ga Shitai with the understanding that, &quot;Everything the characters are experiencing is actually <i>real</i> and Yuuta is a Changeling who has fallen to Banality,&quot; you have one of the better media examples of Changeling that I've seen in a while.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-09T22:05:48-07:00","id":1126969,"title":"","content":"I've been binging on Powered by the Apocalypse stuff recently, myself.<br /> <br /> EXIT: To extrapolate, I'm running some PbtA games for a bunch of newcomers to the hobby, and the ease of play is huge for them! Almost every game can fit the rules onto two pages, and then character creation is literally just grabbing a &quot;class&quot; and hopping right into play.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":281,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","user":"atamajakki","date":". 08-10-2017, 01:58 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-09T22:29:18-07:00","id":1126983,"title":"","content":"Since it's going to be their first time with the setting (which ever one you decide to go with), I would recommend starting with a one-shot, but leaving open the option of continuing to run episodic games with the same characters. For that, I'd say blue book CofD is probably the best way to go. (or Innocents if you prefer - personally, I pretty much see it as blue book CofD with kids.) A good, straightforward modern investigative horror game is a good change of pace from D&amp;D, which seems to be something they're interested in trying, without throwing them in the deep end. The nice thing about playing mortals, whether kids or adults, is that it leaves a clear distinction between the good guys and the monsters, so you don't risk alienating anyone right off the bat. Then if they like it, you can introduce a gameline (any will do really, depending on what interests your group the most.)<br /> <br /> You know what might actually be really fun since your friends are evidently big D&amp;D fans, is to pitch an Innocents game in the style of Stranger Things.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-10T05:19:31-07:00","id":1127057,"title":"","content":"Thanks for your replies.<br /> <br /> I'll probably go for an introductive one-shot and then give them some pitch, so they can decide what they're most interested with. Thanks for the suggestions, you gave me exactly the kind of ideas I was looking for","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/815620-cinder-s-comprehensive-collection-of-creations\" target=\"_blank\">Cinder's Comprehensive Collection of Creations</a> - Homebrew Hub<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1172470-cinder-writes-about-beast-the-primordial\" target=\"_blank\">I write about Beast: The Primordial a lot</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/38-cinder","name":"Cinder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar38_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1408"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}